::BEGIN:LIMITS
# {{prompt.emoji}} Limits
button[Back to Tutorial]: #data help tutorial

::begin:limits
	p: Every limit lives in the {{profile.vector}} vars, with a default beside it. These are the defaults in this version:
	search: 5
	memory: 5
	knowledge: 5
	history: 25
	find: 25
	archive: 3

	p: Search, memory and knowledge take the limit as a param. History has no limit param and uses its vars. Find and archive have vars only, and there is no find or archive command in this version.

	### What counts as a good limit
	p: A limit must be a whole number of 1 or more, written with digits only. `10` is good. `abc`, `0`, `-2`, `2.5` and `10abc` are all bad.

	### What happens with a bad limit
	1. The limit resets to its default, for example 5 for memory.
	2. {{profile.vector}} records the action `incorrect`, the state `invalid` and the intent `bad`, each with the text `[feature]:limit:[value]`, for example `memory:limit:abc`.
	3. The search does not run, and it returns an empty list.
	p: So a bad limit gives no results that time. For memory the text is `no memory`, for knowledge it is the no-knowledge message, and for search and history the data is empty. The next call uses the default.

	### A good limit stays set
	p: A good limit is saved in the vars as a number and stays set for every later call, by any entity, until someone changes it or a bad limit resets it. The standard is to always give the limit you want.
::end:limits

